{"meta":{"course":"inst-design","chapter":"02","section":"05","chapter_title":"ID Process Models","title":"Module 5: Writing Learning Objectives (Bloom + ABCD)","description":"Translate vague stakeholder wishes into testable performances using Bloom's revised taxonomy for cognitive level and Mager's ABCD format for performance specificity.","learning_objectives":[{"id":"inst-design-02-05-lo-01","statement":"Use Bloom's revised taxonomy to name the cognitive demand of a planned learning task"},{"id":"inst-design-02-05-lo-02","statement":"Convert a goal into an ABCD-format objective with audience, behavior, condition, and degree"},{"id":"inst-design-02-05-lo-03","statement":"Audit objectives, assessments, and activities for constructive alignment"}],"concept_ids":["learning_objectives","blooms_revised_taxonomy","mager_abcd","constructive_alignment","performance_objective"],"prereq_section_ids":["inst-design/02/01"],"practice_ids":["objectives_strongest","objectives_bloom_match","objectives_stacking","objectives_constructive_alignment"],"estimated_time_minutes":12,"order":5,"lo_practice_map":{"inst-design-02-05-lo-01":["objectives_bloom_match","objectives_stacking"],"inst-design-02-05-lo-02":["objectives_strongest"],"inst-design-02-05-lo-03":["objectives_constructive_alignment"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# Writing Learning Objectives (Bloom + ABCD)\n\n<figure-block id=\"section-anchor-inst-design-02-05\" caption=\"Learning map for Module 5: Writing Learning Objectives (Bloom + ABCD): principle, evidence, and transfer.\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; repository license\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/section-anchors/inst-design/02-05.svg\" alt=\"Learning map for Module 5: Writing Learning Objectives (Bloom + ABCD), connecting learning objectives, blooms revised taxonomy, mager abcd to evidence and transfer.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\n\n\n> **\"If you're not sure where you're going, you're liable to end up someplace else, and not even know it.\"**\n> - Robert F. Mager, *Preparing Instructional Objectives*\n\n---\n\n## Opening: A Course That Looked Finished but Failed\n\nA learning-design team at a logistics company shipped a four-hour course called *Inventory Fundamentals*. The slides were polished, the narration was professional, and the post-test passed every learner. Six weeks later, the operations manager reported that nothing had changed: warehouse coordinators were still over-ordering and tying up cash in dead stock. When the team audited the course, the problem was upstream of every slide. The single stated objective read: *\"Learners will understand inventory management.\"* The post-test asked learners to match vocabulary terms to definitions. The job required them to compute reorder points from a sales report. The course taught one thing, tested a second, and the job demanded a third, and nobody noticed because the objective was too vague to expose the mismatch.\n\nThat failure is not unusual; it is the default outcome when objectives are written as wishes instead of as testable performances. This section gives you the two tools that prevent it.\n\n> **The Big Picture:** A well-written objective is the contract that binds instruction, assessment, and on-the-job performance together. When the objective is precise, misalignment becomes visible and fixable before a single asset is built. When the objective is vague, the course can look complete and still be broken.\n\n---\n\n## Learning Objectives\n\nBy the end of this section, you will be able to:\n\n1. **Distinguish** an objective from a goal, an outcome, and a content topic\n2. **Classify** the cognitive demand of a task using Bloom's revised taxonomy\n3. **Write** a complete objective in Mager's ABCD format with a measurable degree\n4. **Audit** objectives, assessments, and activities for constructive alignment\n\n---\n\n## Why Objectives Are the Hinge of Instructional Design\n\nLearning objectives sit at the center of every defensible ID decision. They translate vague stakeholder wishes (\"learners should understand inventory management\") into testable performances (\"given a sales-velocity report, the learner will compute the reorder point within 5%\"). Without that translation, the designer cannot align assessments to instruction, and the entire design becomes an act of hope.\n\nObjectives are not the same as **goals**, **outcomes**, or **content topics**. These four terms are routinely confused, and the confusion is expensive because each one drives a different design decision.\n\n| Term | Question it answers | Time horizon | Example |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Goal | What broad direction are we aiming at? | Course or program | \"Improve inventory decisions across the warehouse team.\" |\n| Outcome | What lasting change appears in the learner's work or life? | Months to years | \"Carrying costs drop 15% over two quarters.\" |\n| Content topic | What subject matter is covered? | N/A (it is just material) | \"Reorder points, safety stock, lead time.\" |\n| Objective | What will the learner do, under what conditions, how well? | End of a lesson or module | \"Given a sales-velocity report, compute the reorder point within 5%.\" |\n\n> **Key Insight:** Goals and outcomes justify the project; topics fill the syllabus; only the **objective** is operational enough to write a test item against. Confusing them is the most common reason assessments drift away from instruction.\n\nTwo complementary tools structure most modern objective writing: **Bloom's Revised Taxonomy** (Anderson and Krathwohl, 2001) for cognitive level, and **Mager's ABCD format** for performance specificity. They answer different questions, so competent designers use both. Bloom answers *how deep*; Mager answers *how precisely stated*.\n\n---\n\n## Bloom's Revised Taxonomy: How Deep, Not Just What\n\nThe original Bloom's Taxonomy (1956) ordered cognitive activity from *knowledge* up through *evaluation*. Anderson and Krathwohl revised it in 2001 to use verbs (action) instead of nouns (state), and they swapped the top two levels so that *Create* sits above *Evaluate*. The revised six levels, from least to most demanding, are:\n\n1. **Remember:** recall facts, terms, or basic procedures\n2. **Understand:** explain ideas in your own words; classify, summarize, infer\n3. **Apply:** use a procedure in a familiar situation\n4. **Analyze:** distinguish components, identify causes, compare structures\n5. **Evaluate:** judge claims against criteria, defend a decision\n6. **Create:** produce something new that integrates prior elements\n\nThe taxonomy is most useful when you treat each level as a set of action verbs that commit you to a specific kind of assessment. The decision table below is the one to keep at your desk.\n\n| Level | Sample verbs | What a valid assessment must require | What it is NOT |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Remember | list, name, define, label | Reproduce the fact or term | Explaining why |\n| Understand | explain, classify, summarize, paraphrase | Restate or categorize in the learner's own words | Just reciting the definition |\n| Apply | compute, solve, use, execute | Carry out the procedure on a familiar case | Describing the procedure |\n| Analyze | compare, differentiate, diagnose, attribute | Break a case into parts and find relationships | Applying a single known step |\n| Evaluate | judge, critique, defend, justify | Reach a defended decision against criteria | Listing pros and cons only |\n| Create | design, compose, construct, generate | Produce a novel artifact that integrates elements | Editing a provided template |\n\nTwo design moves follow from the taxonomy.\n\nThe first is **assessment alignment**. If your objective uses the verb *evaluate*, but your assessment item only asks the learner to recall a definition, the assessment is invalid for the objective. The taxonomy lets you spot that mismatch in seconds: read the objective's verb, read the assessment's demand, and confirm they sit at the same row.\n\nThe second is **scaffolding sequence**. Most courses fail not because they aim too low, but because they aim too high too soon. A learner who has not yet **applied** a concept in a familiar case is rarely ready to **evaluate** competing models of it. The taxonomy gives you a defensible order for moving learners up the rows.\n\nPut the six revised levels in order from the least to the most cognitively demanding. This ordering is the default scaffolding sequence.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Remember: recall facts, terms, or basic procedures\",\"Understand: explain ideas in your own words; classify, summarize, infer\",\"Apply: use a procedure in a familiar situation\",\"Analyze: distinguish components, identify causes, compare structures\",\"Evaluate: judge claims against criteria and defend a decision\",\"Create: produce something new that integrates prior elements\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n<inline-check question=\"An objective says 'the learner will critique two competing project plans against the budget criteria and defend which to fund.' Which Bloom level does the verb commit you to, and therefore what must the assessment require?\" explanation=\"'Critique and defend against criteria' is Evaluate. A valid assessment must require a defended judgment against stated criteria, not merely listing pros and cons (which stops short of a defended decision) and not recalling a definition (Remember).\" options='[{\"text\":\"Evaluate: a defended decision against criteria\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Remember: recall the definition of a project plan\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Apply: carry out a single known procedure\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\nA common mistake is treating the levels as a strict ladder where every learner must climb each rung in every lesson. They are not. They are a **vocabulary for naming the cognitive demand** of a given task, so designers and assessors can agree on what the task is actually asking. A single advanced objective (Create) legitimately presumes the lower levels rather than re-teaching them.\n\n<concept-diagram type=\"blooms-revised-taxonomy\"></concept-diagram>\n\n---\n\n## Mager's ABCD Format: Making the Performance Concrete\n\nBloom tells you the cognitive level. Mager tells you the operational shape. Robert Mager's ABCD framework writes an objective as four parts:\n\n- **A, Audience.** Who is the learner? Be specific enough that the reader pictures a real role, not \"students.\"\n- **B, Behavior.** What observable action will they perform? Use a Bloom-aligned action verb. Avoid vague verbs like *know*, *understand*, *be familiar with*, which name a private mental state nobody can observe or score.\n- **C, Condition.** Under what circumstances will the performance happen? What tools, references, data, time limits, or constraints apply? The condition is what makes the objective transferable to a real assessment.\n- **D, Degree.** How well must they perform? What level of accuracy, speed, completeness, or quality counts as success? The degree is the line between pass and fail.\n\nA vague aspiration:\n\n> Learners should understand inventory management.\n\nBecomes, in ABCD form:\n\n> **(A)** Given a 12-month sales-velocity report and a supplier lead-time table, **(B)** the warehouse coordinator will compute the reorder point for any single SKU **(C)** without external help, **(D)** with no more than 5% deviation from the calculated reference value.\n\nThe transformation is not cosmetic. The second version tells you **exactly what to teach, how to assess, and what counts as mastery**. The first version tells you nothing actionable. Notice also how the two tools interlock: the behavior verb *compute* is an Apply-level verb in Bloom, so the matching assessment must hand the learner a real report and require a calculation, not a definition.\n\n---\n\n## Worked Example: Building an Objective End to End\n\nA bank asks for a short course for **newly hired financial analysts** who must read quarterly statements. The stakeholder request arrives as a goal: *\"Analysts should be good at financial ratios.\"* We will convert it into a defensible objective and trace the downstream decisions, step by step.\n\nPredict each conversion step before revealing it.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Converting a vague goal into defensible objectives\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"The stakeholder request is: analysts should be good at financial ratios. What is the FIRST move?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Interview to find the real job task being performed\",\"Write the multiple-choice test\",\"Pick a video platform\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"Name the real job task. Interviews reveal: from a quarterly balance sheet, compute liquidity and leverage ratios and flag any that breach covenant thresholds.\"},{\"prompt\":\"The job has two parts: computing ratios, and flagging breaches against thresholds. What Bloom levels are these?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Compute = Apply; flag against criteria = Evaluate\",\"Both are Remember\",\"Both are Create\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"Computing a ratio from data is Apply; judging a breach against criteria and defending the flag is Evaluate. Two levels is a signal to write TWO objectives, not one stacked one.\"},{\"prompt\":\"For the Apply objective, what must the matching assessment hand the learner?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"An actual balance sheet, requiring the two numbers be computed\",\"A formula-recall multiple-choice item\",\"A definition-matching quiz\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"It must hand over a real balance sheet and require the computed ratios. A ’what is the formula?’ item is Remember-level and therefore invalid for an Apply objective.\"},{\"reveal\":\"The boxed result is not a number but a course skeleton: two objectives produce two aligned assessment formats and two aligned activity types. The original goal could not have produced any of it.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n**Step 1: Name the real job task.** Talking to the team lead reveals the actual performance: from a company's quarterly balance sheet, the analyst must compute liquidity and leverage ratios and flag any that breach the bank's covenant thresholds. That is the performance the objective must capture, not \"be good at ratios.\"\n\n**Step 2: Set the Bloom level.** Computing a ratio from given data is **Apply**. But flagging a covenant breach requires comparing computed values against criteria and reaching a defended judgment, which is **Evaluate**. The job mixes two levels, which is a signal to write two objectives rather than one stacked one.\n\n**Step 3: Write objective 1 in ABCD form (Apply).**\n\n> **(A)** Given a company's quarterly balance sheet, **(B)** the new financial analyst will compute the current ratio and the debt-to-equity ratio, **(C)** using only the provided statement and a calculator, **(D)** with no arithmetic errors.\n\n**Step 4: Write objective 2 in ABCD form (Evaluate).**\n\n> **(A)** Given computed ratios and the bank's covenant threshold table, **(B)** the analyst will judge whether each ratio breaches a covenant and justify the flag in one sentence, **(C)** within a 10-minute review window, **(D)** matching the senior reviewer's determination on at least 9 of 10 cases.\n\n**Step 5: Derive the matching assessment.** Constructive alignment now writes the test for us. Objective 1 (Apply) demands an item that hands the learner an actual balance sheet and asks for the two numbers; a multiple-choice item asking \"what is the formula for the current ratio?\" would be Remember-level and therefore invalid. Objective 2 (Evaluate) demands a case set with ratios plus thresholds where the learner must decide and defend, scored against the 9-of-10 degree.\n\n**Step 6: Derive the matching practice activity.** The verbs dictate practice. *Compute* requires repeated low-stakes calculation drills on real statements; *judge and justify* requires graded case decisions with feedback on the justification, not just the verdict. Reading a chapter about ratios satisfies neither verb.\n\n> **Why this matters:** The boxed result of the whole exercise is not a number; it is a course skeleton. Two objectives produced two aligned assessment formats and two aligned activity types, and every later asset (slides, videos, job aids) now has a target to serve. The original goal \"be good at ratios\" could not have produced any of it.\n\n---\n\n<dynamic-scenario topic=\"Writing Learning Objectives\" context=\"A course author drafts the objective: 'Learners will understand financial ratios.' A peer reviewer asks them to convert it into Bloom + ABCD form for an audience of newly hired financial analysts who will work with quarterly statements.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n---\n\n## Common Objective-Writing Errors\n\nThree patterns cause most weak objectives.\n\n**Verbs that hide cognitive demand.** *Understand*, *appreciate*, *be aware of*, *be familiar with*, and *learn about* are not measurable because they name an internal state with no observable behavior. Replace them with Bloom-aligned verbs: *explain*, *classify*, *compute*, *defend*, *design*, *critique*. A quick test: if you cannot picture the score sheet, the verb is too soft.\n\n**Conditions and degrees that are missing.** Without a condition, the objective is untested in any specific situation, and the assessment writer is free to invent any context, including one the job never presents. Without a degree, you cannot say whether a 60% score, an 80% score, or a 100% score should be considered success, which means \"pass\" is undefined.\n\n**Stacked objectives that hide multiple performances.** \"The learner will analyze and evaluate a clinical case study and design an intervention plan.\" That sentence contains three different cognitive levels (Analyze, Evaluate, Create) and at least three different deliverables. It cannot be cleanly assessed or sequenced. Split it into three objectives, each with its own verb, condition, and degree.\n\n> **Pitfall Watch:** Stacking is the most dangerous of the three because it can look rigorous. The cure is mechanical: one objective, one verb, one deliverable.\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"Take the stacked objective: 'The learner will analyze and evaluate a clinical case study and design an intervention plan.' Explain why this cannot be cleanly assessed or sequenced, then split it into separate clean objectives.\" answer=\"It packs three different Bloom levels (Analyze, Evaluate, Create) and three deliverables into one sentence, so a single score cannot say which performance the learner mastered, and you cannot sequence them because each presumes the one before it. Split into: (1) Analyze the clinical case to identify the contributing factors; (2) Evaluate the candidate interventions against the case criteria and justify a choice; (3) Create an intervention plan integrating the chosen approach. Each now has one verb, one deliverable, and its own assessment.\"></self-explain>\n\n---\n\n## Aligning Objectives, Assessment, and Activities\n\nOnce objectives exist, three downstream decisions become much easier.\n\n- **Assessment design.** Each objective gets at least one assessment item written at its Bloom level. An *Apply* objective demands an item that requires applying the procedure, not one that asks the learner to recall its name.\n- **Activity selection.** Practice activities must give the learner repeated, low-stakes attempts at the objective's verb. If the objective is *design*, the learner needs to design things. Reading about designing is not enough.\n- **Sequencing.** Objectives at lower Bloom levels usually scaffold objectives at higher levels. Map your objectives to a directed graph; that graph becomes your default sequence.\n\nThis three-way alignment is sometimes called **constructive alignment** (Biggs). It is the simplest defect detector in instructional design: where objectives, assessments, and activities disagree about what the learner is supposed to do, the course is broken, regardless of how finished it looks. The opening logistics course failed this test on every axis at once.\n\n---\n\n## Quality Checklist for Each Objective\n\nBefore locking an objective, run this quick audit:\n\n- Does the verb match a single Bloom level?\n- Is the audience specific enough to be tested?\n- Are the conditions of performance explicit?\n- Is the degree of success measurable?\n- Does at least one planned assessment match the verb's cognitive demand?\n- Does at least one practice activity require the same verb?\n\nIf any of these answers are *no*, the objective is not yet ready to drive the design.\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"learning_objectives\",\"blooms_revised_taxonomy\",\"mager_abcd\",\"constructive_alignment\",\"performance_objective\"]'></concept-map>\n\n### Key Frameworks\n\n| Tool | Question it answers | The four/six parts |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Bloom's revised taxonomy | How deep is the cognitive demand? | Remember, Understand, Apply, Analyze, Evaluate, Create |\n| Mager's ABCD | How precisely is the performance stated? | Audience, Behavior, Condition, Degree |\n| Constructive alignment | Do objective, assessment, and activity agree? | Same verb across all three |\n\n### Key Takeaways\n\n- An objective is the only one of goal, outcome, topic, and objective that is operational enough to test against.\n- Bloom names the cognitive level; Mager names the operational shape; you need both.\n- The behavior verb chosen in Mager must sit at the Bloom level you intend, and the assessment must demand that same level.\n- A measurable degree is what separates a pass from a fail; omitting it leaves \"success\" undefined.\n- Split stacked objectives; one verb, one condition, one degree, one deliverable.\n- Constructive alignment is the fastest way to find a broken course before it ships.\n\n---\n\n## What's Next?\n\nIn the next module, we move from writing a single objective to **sequencing a full set of them** into a coherent learning progression, using the Bloom levels and prerequisite relationships to order instruction and to decide where assessment checkpoints belong.\n\n---\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\n<interactive-quiz\n  question=\"Which of the following is the strongest learning objective?\"\n  options='[{\"text\":\"Learners will understand financial ratios.\",\"isCorrect\":false},{\"text\":\"Given a 3-year balance sheet, the analyst will compute the current ratio and the debt-to-equity ratio with no errors.\",\"isCorrect\":true},{\"text\":\"Learners will be familiar with accounting concepts and develop strong intuition.\",\"isCorrect\":false}]'\n  explanation=\"Correct. The strong objective specifies audience (analyst), behavior (compute the ratios), condition (given a 3-year balance sheet), and degree (no errors). The other two rely on unmeasurable verbs.\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## References\n\n- Keller, J. M. (1987). *The systematic process of motivational design.* Performance + Instruction, 26(9), 1–8. https://doi.org/10.1002/pfi.4160260902\n- Bloom, B. S. (1984). *The 2 sigma problem: The search for methods of group instruction as effective as one-to-one tutoring.* Educational Researcher, 13(6), 4–16. https://doi.org/10.3102/0013189X013006004\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"objectives_strongest","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"mager_abcd","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["mager_abcd","learning_objectives"],"stem":"Which of the following is the most useful learning objective for guiding assessment design?","options":["Learners will understand network security.","Given a captured packet trace and an intrusion-detection rulebook, the security analyst will identify all suspicious flows with at least 90% precision and recall.","Learners will appreciate the importance of cybersecurity.","Learners will be familiar with packet analysis."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Audience (security analyst), behavior (identify suspicious flows), condition (given trace and rulebook), and degree (>=90% precision and recall) are all explicit. The others rely on unmeasurable verbs.","misconception_id":"vague_verbs_are_objectives","learning_objective_ids":["inst-design-02-05-lo-02"]},{"id":"objectives_bloom_match","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"blooms_revised_taxonomy","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["blooms_revised_taxonomy","constructive_alignment"],"stem":"An objective uses the verb 'evaluate' but the planned assessment asks the learner only to recall a definition. What is the defect?","options":["There is no defect; recall always supports evaluation.","The assessment does not match the cognitive level of the objective; it tests Remember while the objective claims Evaluate.","The objective should be lowered to Remember.","Bloom's taxonomy does not apply to assessment alignment."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Constructive alignment requires the assessment to test the same cognitive level the objective claims. A recall item cannot validate an Evaluate objective.","misconception_id":"any_test_proves_any_objective","learning_objective_ids":["inst-design-02-05-lo-01"]},{"id":"objectives_stacking","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"performance_objective","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["performance_objective","learning_objectives"],"stem":"An objective reads: 'The learner will analyze and evaluate a case study and design an intervention plan.' Why is this problematic?","options":["It uses too many words.","It stacks three different performances at three different cognitive levels, hiding what success means and what to assess.","It is fine as written.","It uses Bloom verbs, which are not allowed in objectives."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Stacked objectives obscure the assessment target. Split into three: one Analyze, one Evaluate, one Create.","misconception_id":"stacking_is_efficient","learning_objective_ids":["inst-design-02-05-lo-01"]},{"id":"objectives_constructive_alignment","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"constructive_alignment","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["constructive_alignment"],"stem":"What is the central commitment of constructive alignment?","options":["All objectives must come from a textbook.","Objectives, assessment items, and practice activities must agree on what the learner is being asked to do, at the same cognitive level.","Only summative tests count as alignment evidence.","Activities should be harder than assessments."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Constructive alignment (Biggs) is the principle that objectives, assessment, and activities must converge on the same performance.","misconception_id":"alignment_is_a_paperwork_step","learning_objective_ids":["inst-design-02-05-lo-03"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"vague_verbs_are_objectives","pattern":"unmeasurable_verb","description":"Using verbs like understand, appreciate, be familiar with, or be aware of as if they specify a testable performance","trigger":"learner writes 'learners will understand X' as a complete objective","feedback":"Replace unmeasurable verbs with Bloom-aligned action verbs (explain, classify, compute, defend, design, critique). If you cannot test it, you cannot teach to it.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"any_test_proves_any_objective","pattern":"alignment_drift","description":"Believing that any assessment item can validate any objective regardless of cognitive level","trigger":"learner pairs a recall question with an Evaluate-level objective","feedback":"Constructive alignment requires the assessment to operate at the same Bloom level as the objective. A recall item cannot validate an Evaluate or Create objective.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"stacking_is_efficient","pattern":"compound_objective","description":"Combining multiple performances at multiple cognitive levels into a single objective for brevity","trigger":"learner writes 'analyze and evaluate and design' in one objective","feedback":"Each Bloom level deserves its own objective so that assessment, practice, and feedback can be designed precisely. Split stacked objectives into separate, single-verb objectives.","rail_action":"practice"},{"id":"alignment_is_a_paperwork_step","pattern":"compliance_framing","description":"Treating constructive alignment as an end-of-project documentation task rather than a design discipline","trigger":"learner says 'we'll align after we build it'","feedback":"Alignment is the design move that prevents wasted work. Misaligned objectives, activities, and assessments mean parts of the course are teaching or testing the wrong thing entirely.","rail_action":"ask"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"52d2d8d14c0578f745952957e001860ac3414cbdf166e9d8f7050f617ee196a9","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}